  "known_issues_sub": "Current limitations & upstream issues",
  "dlg_known_issues_title": "Known issues",
  "dlg_known_issues_got_it": "Got it",
  "dlg_known_issues_fullscreen_header": "Switching to picture fullscreen (Windows)",
  "dlg_known_issues_fullscreen_body": "Switching between window fullscreen (maximized) and picture fullscreen works around a window_manager native defect: the window is briefly restored from maximized and the switch waits for that restore to finish, so the platform thread never deadlocks. The window may flash back to its floating size during the switch; playback and the fullscreen state itself are unaffected.",
  "dlg_known_issues_thread_header": "Windows thread mode",
  "dlg_known_issues_thread_body": "The UI isolate runs on a separate thread from the platform thread (the engine prints a warning at startup). This avoids a Flutter 3.35+ deadlock when switching window modes.",
  "dlg_known_issues_mediakit_header": "Abnormal exit message (media_kit)",
  "dlg_known_issues_mediakit_body": "On force-close or exit you may see \"Callback invoked after it has been deleted\" and the process abort. This is a known media_kit FFI disposal race (media-kit #1324 / #1340) and does not affect playback itself.",
  "dlg_known_issues_window_close_header": "The process remains briefly after closing the window (Windows)",
  "dlg_known_issues_window_close_body": "On Windows, whether you press Alt + F4, click the title-bar close button, or close the window normally from the system or taskbar menu, every path enters the same graceful shutdown flow. Once the close request is accepted, the window is hidden immediately, so the app appears to close at once; however, the window has not been destroyed yet and the IRIS process remains active briefly in the background to pause playback, save playback progress, release the player and its native callbacks, and finally destroy the window and exit. This may take a few seconds, so IRIS can still appear in Task Manager during that time. This is normal close cleanup and does not mean the close failed; a timed force-exit fallback prevents the process from remaining in the background indefinitely.",
  "dlg_known_issues_vm_scale_header": "Virtual Media merge at large scale",
  "dlg_known_issues_vm_scale_body": "Without any Virtual Media merge rule, even 500,000+ items resolve and play normally (lazy per-page resolution). Once any merge rule is enabled, the queue currently materializes the whole stream on every resolve: up to about 5,000 items is stable (cached); 5,000-10,000 works but with noticeable lag and a full re-walk on every page; above 10,000 deep-position locate and wrap-to-last can fail and every interaction re-walks the full stream; above 100,000 the app can run out of memory on low-end devices; 500,000 will crash. No code change is made for this in the current round; a streaming fix is planned.",
  "dlg_known_issues_vm_scale_fix_header": "Virtual Media merge performance & storage (this update)",
  "dlg_known_issues_vm_scale_fix_body": "Following the entry above, this update rebuilds that path: with merge rules the queue now uses a persisted shared derived index. Structurally, one global order is kept per storage (keyed by sort field, direction and media types) as int32 node ids (~4B per item) and shared by every scenario; each scenario adds only two bitmaps and a sparse overlay - a per-source membership bitmap and an accepted bitmap at 1 bit per item (~61KB each at 500,000 items, ranked in 512-bit blocks with O(1) rank and O(log n) select), plus an overlay that stores only the exceptions (merged group rows with their member lists, placeholder rows, occurrence counts), at no extra cost for ordinary items. Paging and random seek are therefore rank/select lookups over the bitmaps (O(page size) per page, O(log n) per seek) instead of a full stream walk, and shuffle is applied at read time from the seed, so changing it writes nothing. Space is ~5.3B per item per scenario (0.03MB at 5,000, 0.25MB at 50,000, 2.6MB at 500,000), where the old row-based index cost ~74B per member (~37.7MB at 500,000); the old tables are dropped and their space reclaimed. Reference magnitudes (single storage, 10 items merged per item, 50 per page, measured on a dev machine; real numbers vary with device and folder layout): 5,000 items - ~7ms per page, ~3ms per seek; 50,000 - ~7ms, ~2ms; 500,000 - ~8ms, ~3ms, with a full sweep growing memory by ~4-12MB. One-off cost: the first entry, or any rule change, rebuilds the index once (one pass over the whole stream) - about 1s at 5,000 items and 20s at 50,000; the 500,000 case was NOT measured this round because a single build took too long to finish, so no figure is given for it (this is the only remaining cost that still grows with the item count, so expect a wait when first building an index for a very large library). Still worth knowing: virtual-group search still scales with the group count (~0.4s at 5,000 groups, seconds at 50,000), and a combination the shared order cannot express falls back to the previous resolution path - playback still works, at the previous performance. If the behaviour differs clearly from these magnitudes, please contact the appropriate person for a fix.",
  "dlg_known_issues_vm_delete_header": "Deleting files inside a Virtual Media item (desktop)",
  "dlg_known_issues_vm_delete_body": "On desktop, deleting a real file that is part of a merged Virtual Media item can leave the entire merged item unplayable. Until this is fixed, do not use the delete function on physical files that belong to a virtual item.",
  "dlg_known_issues_vm_multiselect_header": "Virtual merged items cannot be multi-selected",
  "dlg_known_issues_vm_multiselect_body": "In the scenario play queue, a merged virtual item (the [vm]-prefixed row) does not take part in multi-select: it shows no checkbox, and bulk operations (exclude, remove from tag, and the keyboard bulk-remove keys) skip it. Tapping to play is unaffected. To exclude or un-tag its media, act on the individual real files that make up the merge instead.",
  "dlg_known_issues_scenario_sort_header": "Scenario play queue: sorting by modified date groups by folder first",
  "dlg_known_issues_scenario_sort_body": "When the scenario play queue has 同目录连续 (same-directory-contiguous) enabled, it sorts by DIRECTORY first and by the selected field second, so a modified-time sort is not one global time order: only files inside the same folder are ordered by time, whereas PotPlayer's playlist sorts the whole list by date. This is why the queue can look differently ordered from PotPlayer. Untick same-directory-contiguous in the sort menu for a flat, whole-list sort. The timestamp is the file's modification time captured at scan time (millisecond precision); entries that were never scanned carry no timestamp and always sort last.",
